Guillaume

We are talking past each other a bit.  I understand you wish to draft your figure from first principles.  However, you must also appreciate you are working with point transect data, hence you do not want to plot the detection function, but rather the probability density function (PDF).  Plotting the PDF is most easily done using mrds:::plot.ds().

Here is a demonstration using the amakihi Hawaiian songbird data from  http://www.creem.st-and.ac.uk/DS.M&A/amakihi/amakihi.csv

library(Distance)
amakihi <- read.csv(file="amakihi.csv")
amakihi$HAS <- as.factor(amakihi$HAS)
ddf.hour.hn <http://ddf.hour.hn> <- ds(data=amakihi, key="hn", adj="cos", formula=~HAS, transect = "point", truncation=82.5)
mrds:::plot.ds(ddf.hour.hn <http://ddf.hour.hn>$ddf,pdf=TRUE)

With HAS==Hour after Sunrise serving as a factor covariate, the shape of the detection function at different levels of this covariate are apparent in the resulting PDF:

That may not be your ultimate solution, but I leave it to others to add to this partial answer.

